  "question": {
    "text": "If \\(\\mathrm{a}_0\\) is denoted as the Bohr radius of hydrogen atom, then what is the de-Broglie wavelength \\((\\lambda)\\) of the electron present in the second orbit of hydrogen atom? [n : any integer]",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "qFGcC",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{8 \\pi a_0}{n}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "8aDJt",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{2 a_0}{n \\pi}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ZwRfv",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{4 \\mathrm{n}}{\\pi \\mathrm{a}_0}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "TK8AF",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{4 \\pi a_0}{n}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$r_n=\\frac{a_0 n^2}{z}$\nAlso, $2 \\pi \\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{n}}=\\mathrm{n} \\lambda$\nWhere $\\lambda$ is de-Broglie wavelength\n$\\frac{2 \\pi \\mathrm{a}_0 \\mathrm{n}^2}{\\mathrm{z}}=\\mathrm{n} \\lambda$\n\nFor second orbit of H -atom\n$\\lambda=\\frac{8 \\pi \\mathrm{a}_0}{\\mathrm{n}}$",
    "image": null
  },
